// Lesson 3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns & Time
// Adds rate limiting to the turret the student built in 3.1 and 3.2. Their
// 3.2 turret fires once per FRAME, because the "player is in range" answer that
// calls Shoot() is re-read inside Update() — a condition, every frame, not an
// event.
//
// Scaffolding: GUIDED (level 2). The shape of the file is given — the student
// has written Update() before, twice — and the two lines carrying the new idea
// are gaps: the tick that spends the clock and the line that refills it.
//
// WHAT CHANGED AND WHY (alignment pass, 2026-11-05): the old page built the
// reference answer out of `Time.time` stamps (`lastAttackTime`, `Time.time >=
// lastAttackTime + cooldown`, `[SerializeField] float cooldown`, and a range
// check with `Vector3.Distance`). The project's TurretController uses NONE of
// that. It uses row (b) — a countdown:
//
//     bool inRange = false;  float timer;  float timeBetweenShots = 1.5f;
//     Start():  timer = timeBetweenShots;
//     Update(): if (inRange) { timer -= Time.deltaTime;
//                               if (timer < 0) { Shoot(); timer = timeBetweenShots; } }
//
// So the table now marks (b) as this game's choice and prices the stamp model
// as the road not taken, and the walkthrough builds (b). The four-row table is
// kept: comparing the approaches is the lesson, and the project's choice is
// only defensible next to the alternatives.
//
// The countdown's real cost is now taught rather than hidden: the tick sits
// inside `if (inRange)`, so the clock FREEZES while the player is outside the
// ring, and a turret remembers a partial cooldown across visits. That is a
// design decision with a visible consequence, and it is in the "Frozen Clock"
// callout.
//
// FACTUAL GROUND TRUTH, do not "fix" these:
//   - Time.deltaTime IS correct in a countdown. The rule that velocity code
//     takes no deltaTime is about Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity, which is already
//     per second. The two rules do not conflict.
//   - no field in this project is `[SerializeField]` except the prefab and
//     spawnPoint wiring. `timeBetweenShots` is a plain float, tuned in the
//     script — the same choice `speed` makes in 1.5.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the socratic block keeps its
// three questions in order (reworded in place, never moved), and the decompose
// block keeps both of its turns (dc1:q1, dc1:q2) and its one gap.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the cooldown. Level 2, so exactly
// one part is withheld — the memory, which is the part with no clean precedent:
// every number this project has remembered so far (a coin count, health,
// whether a door is open) only ever went up or flipped. Today's memory has to
// be spent. Its reading stays attached, because finding the answer in the docs
// is the skill being practised.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'The turret should shoot at me — but not sixty times a second. "Shoot at me, sometimes" ' +
    'is the whole feature, and there is nothing in it yet that a computer can act on.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Know the player is still inside the radius',
      does: 'The answer 3.1 already keeps in `inRange` — set true by `OnTriggerEnter2D()` and false by `OnTriggerExit2D()`. A turret that asks "is the player close?" every frame is re-asking a question the collider already answered.',
      doc: 'the `inRange` field and the two trigger callbacks in your own 3.1 script.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Keep the seconds until the next shot, starting full',
      doc: 'the `Time.deltaTime` row in the quick reference, and the default value rule from 1.3.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
      blank: true,
    },
    {
      name: 'Spend the clock once per frame, and only while it matters',
      does: '`timer -= Time.deltaTime;` inside `if (inRange)`. Each frame hands over its own duration, so after a second of frames the timer is a second smaller — at any frame rate.',
      doc: 'the `Time.deltaTime` row in the quick reference, and the `if` shape from 1.4.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Fire at zero, then refill the whole interval',
      does: '`if (timer < 0)` opens the gate and `timer = timeBetweenShots` closes it again. One line spends, the other resets — the pair is the whole cooldown.',
      doc: 'the **Cooldown** row of the pattern table in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Three of the four parts are code you have already written. The one with no precedent in this ' +
    'course is the memory: a field that is slowly spent rather than counted.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you look this part up on its own, in one search? "Fire less often" cannot be ' +
    'looked up. "Subtract the frame time from a number each frame" can.',
  turns: [
    'The table in the code half prices four ways to time this action. Which did you choose, and what does your choice cost you — in your own words, not the table\'s?',
    'Today\'s memory is spent down to zero and refilled. If the turret had to charge for a second before each shot instead, what would the field mean while it was charging — and which of the four approaches would you reach for?',
  ],
};

// Level 2: the frame of the file is given and the two lines carrying the new
// idea are gaps — the tick and the refill. Every block opens with a comment
// saying where in the file it goes. The `why` on each line is where this
// lesson's mechanism is explained, and it is explained nowhere else: the table,
// the spec and the checklist all point here rather than repeating it.
const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I stop shooting too fast?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/TurretController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'Your 3.1 and 3.2 turret does not change: the trigger still sets `inRange`, and `Shoot()` still spawns a fireball at the muzzle. Today adds a knob, a countdown, and the two lines that spend it.',
  level: 2,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Declare the knob and the countdown',
      lead: 'Both lines go in the class body, outside every method — the placement rule from 1.3.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// TurretController.cs — class body, above Start(), with your other fields.', what: '' },
        { code: '    float timeBetweenShots = 1.5f;', what: 'The knob: how many seconds must pass between shots. A plain `float`, so it is tuned by editing this line and playing again — the same choice `speed` makes in 1.5.', why: '**It has to be a `float`.** An `int` cannot hold `1.5`; it would round to `1`, and a turret tuned to fire twice a second would quietly fire once a second, from a type nobody sees on screen.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    float timer;', what: '**The memory:** one float holding the seconds still owed before this turret may fire again. It shrinks every frame the player is in range, and jumps back to the knob after each shot.', why: '**Where this line lives is the memory.** Move it inside the method that reads it and it is rebuilt from nothing every frame, holding the same empty value each time. That is the scope rule from 1.3: the same line in the wrong place is not a memory, it is a habit.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Start the countdown full',
      lead: 'Once, before any frame has run.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// TurretController.cs — inside Start(), with your 3.1 target setup.', what: '' },
        { code: '        timer = timeBetweenShots;', what: 'The countdown begins at a full interval, exactly as if a shot had just left.', why: '**The first shot is a design decision, not a default.** Starting at `0` would make the turret fire the instant the player crossed the ring — a free shot for a turret the player never got to see. Starting full means every visit costs the same first second.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Spend the countdown while the player is in range',
      lead: 'The `inRange` answer is your 3.1 field. Today it stops being the thing that fires and becomes the thing that lets time run.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// TurretController.cs — inside Update(), on the inRange check from 3.1.', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (inRange)', what: 'The gate is the 3.1 field, unchanged. Everything inside these braces runs only while the player is inside the ring.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            ______________________________', blank: true, what: 'Spend one frame\'s worth of time. Write the line that takes this frame\'s duration off the countdown.' , hint: 'The frame\'s duration is the `Time.deltaTime` row in the quick reference — you multiplied by it in 1.4 to make speed per-second. Here the same number is subtracted instead.' , why: '**This is the whole countdown.** `Time.deltaTime` is the wall-clock seconds since the previous frame — around `0.017` at 60 fps, twice that at 30. Subtract it every frame and after one real second you have subtracted one second, whatever the machine did in between. That is why it is `-=` and not `-= 1`: subtracting `1` would spend the timer in one frame, and the turret would fire every frame again.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '            if (timer < 0)', what: 'The gate: the countdown has run out. `< 0` rather than `== 0` because the tick almost never lands exactly on zero — it steps past it. "At or past empty" is what `< 0` says.' },
        { code: '            {', what: '' },
        { code: '                Shoot();', what: 'Your firing method from 3.2 — the one that spawns a fireball at the muzzle and hands it `target`. Unchanged, whatever you named it.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '                ______________________________', blank: true, what: 'Refill the countdown so the next shot costs the same interval again. This runs only when a shot has actually left the barrel.' , hint: 'The field holds seconds remaining, and the knob holds how many seconds a full interval is. What assignment puts it back to full?' , why: '**The most common bug in this lesson.** Leave it out and the timer keeps going negative: the gate stays open on the next frame and every frame after, and the turret is back to firing at your frame rate — the bug you started with, rebuilt inside the fix.' },
        { code: '            }', what: '' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class TurretController : MonoBehaviour
{
    float timeBetweenShots = 1.5f;      // the knob: seconds between shots
    float timer;                        // the memory: seconds still owed

    bool inRange = false;               // set by the Detection Radius trigger
    Transform target;                   // who walked in

    [SerializeField] GameObject fireballPrefab;
    [SerializeField] Transform spawnPoint;

    void Start()
    {
        // ...your 3.1 detection and targeting stays as it is...

        timer = timeBetweenShots;       // a full interval before the first shot
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (inRange)
        {
            timer -= Time.deltaTime;    // spend one frame's worth of time

            if (timer < 0)              // at or past empty
            {
                Shoot();

                timer = timeBetweenShots;   // refill: the gate closes again
            }
        }
    }

    void Shoot()
    {
        // ...the projectile spawn you wrote in 3.2, unchanged...
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it: your names, your interval, your spawn and your 3.1 trigger are yours, and the shape is what has to match. If you built the `Time.time` stamp instead, you have implemented row (a) — a real answer, and not the one this project chose.',
};

const body = `<h1>3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Last lesson your turret did not fire shots, it fired a <em>stream</em> — every frame, for as long
    as you stood in range. Today it learns to wait, using a number that gets spent.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I stop shooting too fast?', 2)}
${renderDecompose('3.3', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and stand inside one turret's radius for five
            seconds, then <strong>Console →</strong> count the lines that appear. Write the number
            down: it is the rate your frames are running at, not a decision anybody made.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts/TurretController.cs</code>, then
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and watch the Scene view: the fireballs leave the
            muzzle in one clump per frame, faster than you can read.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the walkthrough is done — Project window → TurretController.cs</strong>, set
            <code>timeBetweenShots</code> to <code>2</code>, save, and press <strong>Play</strong>,
            then time ten shots against a stopwatch. It is a <code>float</code> in the script, so
            tuning is an edit and a new play — the same rule as <code>speed</code> in
            <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>, not an Inspector box.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → the two Turret instances</strong>, and stand between them: with the
            interval in the script, both turrets keep time identically. If you want one to be slower,
            you need a second value — see the callout in the code half.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Pick a Timing Model</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Approach</th>
            <th>What you store</th>
            <th>The gate</th>
            <th>What it costs you</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(a) A <code>Time.time</code> timestamp</strong></td>
            <td>One float: the moment the last shot happened.</td>
            <td><code>Time.time &gt;= lastShot + interval</code></td>
            <td>The clock runs whether or not the turret cares, so a player who leaves and comes back
                ten seconds later is shot immediately — the cooldown paid itself while nobody was
                there.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(b) A countdown timer</strong> — <em>this project</em></td>
            <td>One float: seconds <em>remaining</em> until the next shot is allowed.</td>
            <td><code>timer &lt; 0</code>, then <code>timer = interval</code></td>
            <td>Something has to tick it down, every frame, and you have to decide what the tick
                means outside the radius. In exchange it reads as "seconds until I can shoot", which
                is what you want to see while tuning pacing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(c) <code>InvokeRepeating</code></strong></td>
            <td>Nothing at all — the engine holds the schedule.</td>
            <td><code>InvokeRepeating(nameof(Shoot), 0f, interval)</code></td>
            <td>Almost no code, and that is the trap: your method is called on cadence whether or not
                the player is in range, so the range check has to move inside it. Cancelling is by
                method <em>name</em>, so a rename breaks the cancel silently.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(d) A coroutine loop</strong></td>
            <td>A running coroutine handle.</td>
            <td><code>while (true) { yield return new WaitForSeconds(t); }</code></td>
            <td>A <code>StartCoroutine()</code> call, a stored handle to stop it, and a second mental
                model of when your code runs. It is the only row that expresses "charge, then burst,
                then rest" cleanly.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Why this project takes (b).</strong> The turret's trigger already tells it when the
        player is in range, and row (b) is the only one that can be ticked <em>inside</em> that
        answer. The cost is the callout below, and it is a real one: the two rows do not behave the
        same when the player walks away mid-cooldown.
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The frozen clock.</strong> The tick sits inside <code>if (inRange)</code>, so the
        countdown <em>stops</em> while the player is outside the ring. Leave at 0.8 seconds remaining
        and come back ten minutes later, and the turret still owes 0.8 seconds. Row (a) would not do
        that — its clock never stops. Neither behaviour is wrong; one is a turret that saves your
        progress and the other is a turret that reloads on its own. Know which yours is, because a
        player will notice.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Row</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification — for whichever row you chose.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The knob.</strong> Which float sets the gap between shots, and what default will you ship for it? A plain field is tuned in the script; <code>[SerializeField]</code> would put it in the Inspector so one turret could differ from another. Say which you used and what you gave up.</li>
            <li><strong>The memory.</strong> What does <em>your</em> row keep between frames — a moment, a duration, a handle, or nothing the engine is not already holding — and why can it not live inside the method that reads it?</li>
            <li><strong>The gate and the reset.</strong> Which <em>two</em> conditions must both be true before the turret may fire, and which of them comes from a trigger callback rather than a comparison? What changes at the instant a shot actually leaves?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choosing a Timing Model</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Trace your own 3.2 build: standing inside the radius for one second, how many projectile clones did your firing method create — and why did that number depend on the machine rather than on you?</li>
<li>Why can't you call a "wait one second" function inside <code>Update()</code>? Describe what the player would see on screen during that wait, and explain why a game engine refuses to let you block the frame.</li>
<li>Row (a) stores a <em>moment</em>; row (b) stores a <em>duration</em>. Which one can you print in the Console and have a player immediately understand, and which one is easier to compare against a designer's tuning value? Then say which this project took, and what it gave up to get there.</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

